course: dictionary
lesson: 11616
title: Appropriate: right for the moment
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Appropriate is an adjective. It means right or suitable for a particular situation, person or time. Ask yourself: is this the right thing for this moment? «Wear appropriate clothes for the rain» means clothes that suit wet weather, like a coat and boots.
explain: Look at the words around it for clues. «Please use an appropriate tone when you speak to the teacher» means a polite tone that suits school, not a shout. Try your meaning in the sentence: "a suitable tone" still makes sense, so you have it.
explain: Appropriate often comes in phrases: appropriate for, appropriate to, and where appropriate. «The film is appropriate for children your age» means it suits children your age. «Add a heading where appropriate» means add one only when it fits.
explain: Appropriate can also be a formal verb with different meanings. «The council may appropriate the empty field» means take it for its own use without permission. «The club will appropriate £50 for new footballs» means set that money aside for a special use. In a school text, the adjective is the usual meaning.
fact: Appropriate means right or suitable for a particular situation, person or time.
fact: As an adjective, its comparative is more appropriate.
fact: Useful phrases: appropriate for, appropriate to, and where appropriate.
fact: As a formal verb, to appropriate means to take something for yourself, often without permission.
fact: As a formal verb, to appropriate money means to set it aside for a special use.
exercise: «Please wear appropriate clothing for the hike.» What does appropriate mean here? | Very expensive | Brand new and shiny | Too big to wear | *Suitable for the walk in the hills
exercise: «Use an appropriate unit for the length of the pool.» In maths, which unit is appropriate for a pool's length? | Grams | Seconds | Litres | *Metres
exercise: «Where appropriate, add a heading.» What does where appropriate mean? | Nowhere at all | Only in the morning | *Where it fits or suits | Everywhere, all the time
test: «Sam's behaviour in the library was appropriate.» What does appropriate mean? | Very loud | Lost | *Right and suitable for the place | Rude and noisy
test: «The council may appropriate the empty field.» What does appropriate mean here? | To give it away to a charity | To paint it bright colours | To measure it carefully | *To take it for its own use, often without permission
exam: «The club will appropriate £50 for new footballs.» What does appropriate mean here? | *Set aside for a special use | Lost it | Took it without asking | Spent it all on sweets
